WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
You may have seen a fuzzy patch spreading

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:03.360
across old bread.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.640
That patch is a fungus.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.760
But how could it start growing on another

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.560
piece of bread without the two pieces touching?

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.680
Let’s follow one example, a bread mould called

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.360
Rhizopus stolonifer.

00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:18.886
Close up, the mould is made of thin

00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:20.366
branching threads.

00:00:20.647 --> 00:00:23.446
One thread is called a hypha; several are

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:24.086
hyphae.

00:00:24.647 --> 00:00:27.206
These threads grow over and into the bread.

00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:30.006
The fungus gets nutrients from the bread, which

00:00:30.087 --> 00:00:31.126
support its growth.

00:00:31.687 --> 00:00:34.406
The fuzzy patch is a growing fungal body,

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:36.566
rather than a pile of loose spores.

00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:41.326
Some threads form upright stalks with rounded heads.

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:44.806
Inside a head, the fungus produces many tiny

00:00:44.967 --> 00:00:47.206
reproductive cells called spores.

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:50.246
A spore can begin new fungal growth.

00:00:50.887 --> 00:00:53.526
The rounded structure where these spores form is

00:00:53.687 --> 00:00:54.886
called a sporangium.

00:00:55.447 --> 00:00:58.806
So the sporangium makes the spores; the spores

00:00:58.887 --> 00:01:00.566
are the cells that can begin a new

00:01:00.647 --> 00:01:01.446
fungus.

00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:05.946
When the sporangium matures, its wall breaks down

00:01:06.027 --> 00:01:07.626
and spores can be released.

00:01:08.187 --> 00:01:10.106
Air movement can carry them away from the

00:01:10.187 --> 00:01:11.066
parent mould.

00:01:11.627 --> 00:01:13.706
This allows a spore to reach another food

00:01:13.787 --> 00:01:16.666
surface, even when no fungal thread joins the

00:01:16.747 --> 00:01:17.466
two places.

00:01:18.187 --> 00:01:20.666
Arriving is only the first step.

00:01:20.707 --> 00:01:22.986
The spore still needs conditions that allow it

00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:23.786
to grow.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.640
On suitable food, with enough moisture and a

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.760
suitable temperature, the spore can begin to grow.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:32.920
We call that germination.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.120
A thin thread grows out from the spore,

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.040
then branches to build a new fungal body.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.480
In the production route we have shown, the

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.440
parent mould made the spores without reproductive cells

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:45.480
joining.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.120
That is asexual reproduction.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.240
Rhizopus also has a sexual route, so finding

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.000
a spore on its own would not tell

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.360
us how it was produced.

00:01:55.567 --> 00:01:57.206
Here is a new arrangement.

00:01:57.487 --> 00:02:00.446
Bread A has Rhizopus mould with mature spore

00:02:00.527 --> 00:02:01.166
cases.

00:02:01.727 --> 00:02:03.966
Bread B has no mould initially.

00:02:04.527 --> 00:02:06.926
The pieces do not touch, but share moving

00:02:07.007 --> 00:02:07.406
air.

00:02:08.047 --> 00:02:10.926
B is moist, supplies nutrients and is at

00:02:11.007 --> 00:02:12.126
a suitable temperature.

00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:15.086
How could mould reach B, and what would

00:02:15.167 --> 00:02:16.286
begin growing there?

00:02:16.847 --> 00:02:19.006
Pause and explain the two steps.

00:02:34.434 --> 00:02:36.633
The pieces do not need to touch.

00:02:36.914 --> 00:02:40.113
Mature spore cases on A can release spores,

00:02:40.194 --> 00:02:43.153
and moving air can carry some towards B.

00:02:43.234 --> 00:02:45.393
If a spore lands on B, the moist

00:02:45.474 --> 00:02:47.953
bread provides water and nutrients.

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:51.153
At the stated suitable temperature, it can germinate:

00:02:51.234 --> 00:02:54.033
a fungal thread begins to grow, then branches

00:02:54.154 --> 00:02:55.313
into a new colony.

00:02:56.534 --> 00:02:59.893
That gives us a useful reminder: threads feed,

00:02:59.974 --> 00:03:01.253
spores spread.

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:03.973
The threads get nutrients where the fungus is

00:03:04.054 --> 00:03:04.613
growing.

00:03:05.014 --> 00:03:07.413
The spores can carry this reproductive route to

00:03:07.494 --> 00:03:08.453
a new place.

00:03:09.094 --> 00:03:11.573
In the route we followed, the parent produces

00:03:11.654 --> 00:03:15.013
spores without reproductive cells joining, so it is

00:03:15.174 --> 00:03:16.813
asexual reproduction.

00:03:17.174 --> 00:03:19.333
Next, we will see how one yeast cell

00:03:19.414 --> 00:03:21.173
makes another by budding.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.960
If you would like notes, copy these now,

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.720
or pause for more time.
